WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.360
When a receptor grips a ligand that is stuck to another cell, which cell gets the

2
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.780
message? The receptor sits in the upper cell.

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:16.420
Its ligand is held in the membrane of the lower cell.

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:19.580
Does the message reach one of them, or both?

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.580
Often, both.

6
00:00:22.483 --> 00:00:25.583
Many ligands are made as membrane proteins.

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:29.643
Such a ligand can have a tail inside its own cell.

8
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:33.643
A tail in the cytosol is what you expect of a receptor.

9
00:00:34.643 --> 00:00:38.883
When the two proteins bind, the receptor signals into its own cell.

10
00:00:39.443 --> 00:00:40.943
That is the forward signal.

11
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:44.983
A second message passes into the cell that carries the ligand.

12
00:00:45.363 --> 00:00:46.743
That is reverse signaling.

13
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:52.277
Ephrins are the clearest case of reverse signaling.

14
00:00:53.017 --> 00:00:57.597
A B-type ephrin crosses the membrane once and ends in a short tail.

15
00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.177
The tail contains no enzyme.

16
00:01:00.697 --> 00:01:05.757
Yet when the ephrin meets the outer part of its receptor, its tail gains a phosphate

17
00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:10.857
on a tyrosine. That outer part has no kinase, and neither does the ephrin.

18
00:01:10.857 --> 00:01:15.017
So a kinase in the ephrin's own cell must do the work.

19
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:22.650
Which kinase? Src-family kinases gather where B-type ephrins cluster, and they

20
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.590
help to put phosphate on the tail.

21
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.670
An adaptor then binds the phosphorylated tyrosines.

22
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.930
It connects the ephrin to proteins that reshape the actin skeleton.

23
00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:38.010
So a tail with no enzyme of its own borrows one from its cell.

24
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:46.147
Suppose you delete the gene for a B-type ephrin and an organ fails to form.

25
00:01:46.867 --> 00:01:51.827
You have removed the forward signal, the reverse signal, and the grip that held the

26
00:01:51.827 --> 00:01:56.867
cells together. The knockout cannot tell you which loss caused the defect.

27
00:01:57.627 --> 00:01:59.507
So change the ephrin instead.

28
00:02:00.247 --> 00:02:04.547
Keep its outer part and its membrane segment, and remove only the tail.

29
00:02:04.547 --> 00:02:10.047
If the altered protein still reaches the cell surface, the grip and the forward

30
00:02:10.047 --> 00:02:12.807
signal stay. The reverse signal is gone.

31
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.940
Other ligands work this way too.

32
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.820
Membrane-bound TNF acts as a receptor, and so does a fly semaphorin.

33
00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:27.840
In each pair, the protein named receptor also gives a signal.

34
00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.040
The protein named ligand also receives one.

35
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.280
The names were fixed by history.

36
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.000
So keep this rule: a membrane ligand can receive a signal as well as give one.
